<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Punic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">149–<a href="/years/146-bce">146 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Punic War was the last conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, fought from 149 to 146 BCE. After a long siege, Roman forces captured and destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE, and its territory became the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> treat the war as the final destruction of the Carthaginian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-punic-war.jpg" alt="Third Punic War" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harrias · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Carthage_circa_149_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> ended in 201 BCE with a treaty that stripped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> of its overseas territories and some African ones, and forbade it to wage war outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> or, within Africa, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s express permission. Carthage was thereafter politically subordinate to Rome. Masinissa, King of Numidia and an ally of Rome, repeatedly raided and seized Carthaginian land over the following fifty years, and whenever Carthage appealed to Rome for redress or permission to act, Rome backed Masinissa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> then sent an army under Hasdrubal against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>, treaty or no treaty, and it ended in disaster at the Battle of Oroscopa. A faction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> had long wanted further action against Carthage, among them Cato, whom later tradition credits with ending every speech with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-delenda-est">Carthago delenda est</a>. The illicit campaign gave them a pretext.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 149 BCE a large Roman army landed at Utica under the consuls Manius Manilius and Lucius Marcius Censorinus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> tried to appease them and handed over their weapons, including a recorded 200,000 sets of armour and 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>. Censorinus then demanded that they abandon the city and settle 16 kilometres from the sea. They refused and prepared to defend Carthage. The Roman siege suffered repeated setbacks through 149 and 148 BCE, relieved only by the conduct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>, then a tribune.</p><p>In 147 BCE public support for Scipio was so strong that the age limits were set aside and he was elected consul and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He built a large mole to cut off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> runners. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> cut a new channel to the sea and sortied with a rebuilt fleet, but many of their ships were sunk or captured on the withdrawal. Scipio then overran the Carthaginian field army's camp at Nepheris, and most of the towns still holding out surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In spring 146 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> breached the walls, and over six days they worked through the residential quarter, killing everyone they met and burning the buildings behind them. Only on the last day were prisoners taken, 50,000 of whom were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The notion that the site was sown with salt is likely a 19th-century invention. A curse was laid on anyone who resettled the site, which became public land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s territory became the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with Utica as its capital.</p><p>Punic culture, language and religion survived in the surviving cities, and the Punic language was still spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> until the 7th century CE. Augustus began rebuilding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> as a Roman city in 29 BCE. Polybius, the main source, was with Scipio and so presents him favourably.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Punic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Roman Republic destroyed Carthage at the end of the <b>Third Punic War</b> in 146 BCE, it turned the city's remaining territory in what is now northern Tunisia into the province of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…to secure their position: Mithridates II married a Seleucid princess, Mithridates V sent help to Rome in the <b>Third Punic War</b> and against Aristonicus in Asia and was rewarded with Phrygia, which Rome later withdrew, and Mithridates VI married his daughter Cleopatra to Tigranes the Great of Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 150 BCE Carthage finally fought back and was defeated, and its breach of the treaty gave Rome its pretext for the <b>Third Punic War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cato the Elder is said to have ended his speeches with the declaration that Carthage must be destroyed (Carthago delenda est), and the <b>Third Punic War</b> of 149–146 BCE was a siege of a city that posed little real threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Third Punic War</b> Utica sided with Rome, which made it a free city and the capital of the new province of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/utica">Utica</a></li></ul></section>
